{
 "meta": {
  "title": "Manual for English Users — Thai sounds & romanization",
  "note": "표준(RTGS) vs 우리 로마자. ours 필드의 {..} = 장음(앱에서 밑줄 렌더링)."
 },
 "intro": "The standard romanization of Thai (RTGS, used on road signs and in dictionaries) is quite far from how Thai actually sounds: it writes both \"g\" and \"k\" as k, both \"bp\" and \"p\" as p, and it hides the difference between short and long vowels and between \"o\" (go) and \"aw\" (law). This app therefore uses its own romanization that is much easier to read as an English speaker. Long vowels are shown with an underline instead of doubling letters (m̲a̲ = long a, not maa). Tones, when shown, use accent marks on the vowel: à low, â falling, á high, ǎ rising.",
 "legend": [
  "g / dt / bp = unaspirated k / t / p (no puff of air) — the RTGS k / t / p",
  "k / t / p = aspirated (with a puff of air) — the RTGS kh / th / ph",
  "j = RTGS ch for จ; ch = RTGS ch for ฉ ช ฌ",
  "aw = the \"law\" vowel (RTGS o); o = the \"go\" vowel (RTGS o)",
  "eu = flat-lipped \"oo\" (RTGS ue); er = \"her\" without the r (RTGS oe)",
  "underline = long vowel"
 ],
